IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `roc-core`

-- Rules of Court 2021 (Singapore), G.N. No. S 914/2021, current version as at 5 November 2026.
-- ORDER 59: PROCEEDINGS BY AND AGAINST GOVERNMENT (13 rules): how the ordinary rules are changed when the
-- Government sues or is sued under the Government Proceedings Act.
-- Source: a plain-text rendering made by a converter script from Singapore Statutes Online.
-- It is not the official text, and nothing here has been checked against the official text.
--
-- HOW TO READ A RESULT.  A MAYBE BOOLEAN answer is:
--   JUST TRUE   the text says yes on these facts.
--   JUST FALSE  the text says no in its own words (the FALSE-kind comment quotes them).
--   NOTHING     the text does not answer on these facts (the NOTHING-kind comment says why).
-- A caller must never turn NOTHING into FALSE.
-- O 3 r 2(1) is a caveat on every answer: every requirement in the Rules is subject to the Court's
-- discretion to order otherwise in the interests of justice; it is not threaded as an input.
-- What follows non-compliance with a Rule is O 3 r 2(4); the functions below that say NOTHING for a "must"
-- not done cite that provision.
--
-- BORROWED FACTS.  The Government Proceedings Act is not among the sources.  Its sections (2(3), 19(4), 20, 21,
-- 31(1), 33(1), 34(1), 35(2)) and Parts III and V are named in the inputs and asked as facts.
-- Other Orders this Order changes (Order 6 rules 5, 8 and 14, Order 7, Order 8, Order 9 rules 17 and 19, Order 10,
-- Order 13 rule 9, Order 22, Order 23) are in other parts: what they say is not here (S-L1/S-L2), and a
-- function that overrides one is a flat statement about that provision and carries a FALSE-kind comment.
--
-- TIME.  O 3 r 3(1) says the Interpretation Act does not apply to the calculation of time in these Rules.
-- Whether an act was in time is a comparison with an input `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act`
-- (seam S-T1: roc1's O 3 r 3 computes it, and the merged file wires it).  The time limited for filing and
-- serving a notice of intention to contest or not contest is O 6's (seam: `o 6 r 6 the last day ...`).

-- ===========================================================================
§ `Order 59 -- proceedings by and against Government`
-- ===========================================================================

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Rule 1 -- application and definitions of this Order`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:6906 | 1. —(1) These Rules apply to civil proceedings to which the Government is a party subject to the following Rules. (2) In this Order — “Act” means the Government Proceedings Act and any reference to a section is a reference to a section in the Act;
@ref O 59 r 1(1) (src:6906)
GIVEN `the proceedings are civil proceedings to which the Government is a party` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 1(1) these Rules apply to the proceedings` MEANS
    IF `the proceedings are civil proceedings to which the Government is a party`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "These Rules apply to civil proceedings to which the Government is a party" names the proceedings it reaches and says nothing of others

-- src:6906 | 1. —(1) These Rules apply to civil proceedings to which the Government is a party subject to the following Rules. (2) In this Order — “Act” means the Government Proceedings Act and any reference to a section is a reference to a section in the Act;
@ref O 59 r 1(2) "Act" (src:6906)
`o 59 r 1(2) the Act` MEANS "the Government Proceedings Act"

-- "any reference to a section is a reference to a section in the Act".  The text carves out no reference that
-- names another Act expressly; it is silent on one.
-- src:6906 | 1. —(1) These Rules apply to civil proceedings to which the Government is a party subject to the following Rules. (2) In this Order — “Act” means the Government Proceedings Act and any reference to a section is a reference to a section in the Act;
@ref O 59 r 1(2) (src:6906)
GIVEN `the reference is to a section` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the reference names another Act expressly` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 1(2) a reference to a section is a reference to a section in the Act` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the reference names another Act expressly`
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the Rule does not say what a reference does that names another Act
        IF `the reference is to a section`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "any reference to a section" speaks only of a reference to a section

-- The three "civil proceedings by / against / by or against the Government" are defined by Part III of the Act;
-- "civil proceedings to which the Government is a party" is defined as for Part V by section 2(3).  The meanings are in the Act.
-- src:6907 | “civil proceedings by the Government”, “civil proceedings against the Government” and “civil proceedings by or against the Government” have the meanings given by Part III of the Act;
@ref O 59 r 1(2) "civil proceedings by the Government" (src:6907)
`o 59 r 1(2) "civil proceedings by the Government" the part of the Act that gives its meaning` MEANS "Part III of the Act"

-- src:6907 | “civil proceedings by the Government”, “civil proceedings against the Government” and “civil proceedings by or against the Government” have the meanings given by Part III of the Act;
@ref O 59 r 1(2) "civil proceedings by the Government" (src:6907)
`o 59 r 1(2) "civil proceedings against the Government" the part of the Act that gives its meaning` MEANS "Part III of the Act"

-- src:6907 | “civil proceedings by the Government”, “civil proceedings against the Government” and “civil proceedings by or against the Government” have the meanings given by Part III of the Act;
@ref O 59 r 1(2) "civil proceedings by the Government" (src:6907)
`o 59 r 1(2) "civil proceedings by or against the Government" the part of the Act that gives its meaning` MEANS "Part III of the Act"

-- src:6908 | “civil proceedings to which the Government is a party” has the same meaning as it has for the purposes of Part V of the Act, by virtue of section 2(3);
@ref O 59 r 1(2) "civil proceedings to which the Government is a party" (src:6908)
`o 59 r 1(2) "civil proceedings to which the Government is a party" the provision that gives its meaning` MEANS "Part V of the Act, by virtue of section 2(3)"

-- "order" includes a judgment, decree, rule, award or declaration.
DECLARE `o 59 r 1(2) kind of thing` IS ONE OF
    `o 59 r 1(2) a judgment`
    `o 59 r 1(2) a decree`
    `o 59 r 1(2) a rule`
    `o 59 r 1(2) an award`
    `o 59 r 1(2) a declaration`
    `o 59 r 1(2) another kind of thing`

-- src:6909 | “order” includes a judgment, decree, rule, award or declaration;
@ref O 59 r 1(2) "order" (src:6909)
GIVEN kind IS A `o 59 r 1(2) kind of thing`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 1(2) "order" the thing is within the term` MEANS
    CONSIDER kind
    WHEN `o 59 r 1(2) another kind of thing` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (includes): "includes a judgment, decree, rule, award or declaration" does not close the list
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- "order against the Government" means any order (including an order for costs) made in any civil proceedings by or
-- against the Government, or in connection with any arbitration to which the Government is a party, in favour of any
-- person against the Government or against a Government department or against an officer of the Government as such.
-- A "means" definition, so a case outside it is JUST FALSE.
-- src:6910 | “order against the Government” means any order (including an order for costs) made in any civil proceedings by or against the Government, or in connection with any arbitration to which the Government is a party, in favour of any person against the Government or against a Government department or against an officer of the Government as such.
@ref O 59 r 1(2) "order against the Government" (src:6910)
GIVEN `the order is made in civil proceedings by or against the Government` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the order is made in connection with an arbitration to which the Government is a party` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the order is in favour of a person against the Government, a Government department or an officer of the Government as such` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 1(2) "order against the Government" the order is an order against the Government` MEANS
    IF (`the order is made in civil proceedings by or against the Government` OR `the order is made in connection with an arbitration to which the Government is a party`) AND `the order is in favour of a person against the Government, a Government department or an officer of the Government as such`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "means any order (including an order for costs) made in any civil proceedings by or against the Government, or in connection with any arbitration to which the Government is a party, in favour of any person against the Government or against a Government department or against an officer of the Government as such"

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Rule 2 -- particulars to be included in the endorsement of claim`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DECLARE `o 59 r 2(1) endorsement of claim` HAS
    `o 59 r 2(1) states the circumstances in which the Government's liability is alleged to have arisen` IS A BOOLEAN
    `o 59 r 2(1) states the Government department concerned` IS A BOOLEAN
    `o 59 r 2(1) states the officers of the Government concerned` IS A BOOLEAN

-- src:6912 | 2. —(1) In the case of an originating claim which begins civil proceedings against the Government the endorsement of claim required by Order 6, Rule 5 must include a statement of the circumstances in which the Government’s liability is alleged to have arisen and as to the Government department and officers of the Government concerned. (2) If in civil proceedings against the Government a defendant considers that the originating claim does not contain a sufficient statement as required by this Rule, the defendant may, before the expiration of the time limited for filing and serving a notice of intention to contest or not contest, apply to the claimant by notice for a further and better statement containing such information as may be specified in the notice.
@ref O 59 r 2(1) (src:6912)
GIVEN endorsement IS A `o 59 r 2(1) endorsement of claim`
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`o 59 r 2(1) the matters the endorsement has not yet stated` MEANS
    concat (LIST
        (IF endorsement's `o 59 r 2(1) states the circumstances in which the Government's liability is alleged to have arisen` THEN EMPTY ELSE LIST "the circumstances in which the Government's liability is alleged to have arisen"),
        (IF endorsement's `o 59 r 2(1) states the Government department concerned` THEN EMPTY ELSE LIST "the Government department concerned"),
        (IF endorsement's `o 59 r 2(1) states the officers of the Government concerned` THEN EMPTY ELSE LIST "the officers of the Government concerned"))

-- src:6912 | 2. —(1) In the case of an originating claim which begins civil proceedings against the Government the endorsement of claim required by Order 6, Rule 5 must include a statement of the circumstances in which the Government’s liability is alleged to have arisen and as to the Government department and officers of the Government concerned. (2) If in civil proceedings against the Government a defendant considers that the originating claim does not contain a sufficient statement as required by this Rule, the defendant may, before the expiration of the time limited for filing and serving a notice of intention to contest or not contest, apply to the claimant by notice for a further and better statement containing such information as may be specified in the notice.
@ref O 59 r 2(1) (src:6912)
GIVEN `the originating claim begins civil proceedings against the Government` IS A BOOLEAN
      endorsement IS A `o 59 r 2(1) endorsement of claim`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 2(1) the endorsement of claim states what the Rule requires` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the originating claim begins civil proceedings against the Government`)
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): rule 2(1) speaks of "an originating claim which begins civil proceedings against the Government"
        IF count (`o 59 r 2(1) the matters the endorsement has not yet stated` endorsement) EQUALS 0
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): a bare "must include a statement"; QB: NOTHING (O 3 r 2(4) says what the Court may do about non-compliance) / other: JUST FALSE

-- (2): the defendant's application for a further and better statement.  "If in civil proceedings against the Government
-- a defendant considers ..." is a condition (JUST FALSE when unmet); "before the expiration of the time limited" is a period
-- the text fixes in its own words (JUST FALSE outside it).
-- src:6912 | 2. —(1) In the case of an originating claim which begins civil proceedings against the Government the endorsement of claim required by Order 6, Rule 5 must include a statement of the circumstances in which the Government’s liability is alleged to have arisen and as to the Government department and officers of the Government concerned. (2) If in civil proceedings against the Government a defendant considers that the originating claim does not contain a sufficient statement as required by this Rule, the defendant may, before the expiration of the time limited for filing and serving a notice of intention to contest or not contest, apply to the claimant by notice for a further and better statement containing such information as may be specified in the notice.
@ref O 59 r 2(2) (src:6912)
GIVEN `the proceedings are civil proceedings against the Government` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the defendant considers that the originating claim does not contain a sufficient statement as required by this Rule` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the date the defendant applies by notice` IS A DATE
      `o 6 r 6 the last day for filing and serving a notice of intention to contest or not contest` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 2(2) the defendant may apply to the claimant by notice for a further and better statement` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the proceedings are civil proceedings against the Government` AND `the defendant considers that the originating claim does not contain a sufficient statement as required by this Rule`)
        THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "If in civil proceedings against the Government a defendant considers that the originating claim does not contain a sufficient statement as required by this Rule"
        IF `the date the defendant applies by notice` AT MOST `o 6 r 6 the last day for filing and serving a notice of intention to contest or not contest`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "before the expiration of the time limited for filing and serving a notice of intention to contest or not contest"

`o 59 r 2(3)(a) the period after the defendant's written notification` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 4
    `period unit` IS Days
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

`o 59 r 2(3)(b) the period after the Court's decision` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 4
    `period unit` IS Days
    `period direction` IS `After the anchor`

-- (3): "Where a defendant gives a notice under this Rule, the time limited ... does not expire until (a) 4 days after the
-- defendant has notified the claimant in writing that the defendant is satisfied ...; or (b) 4 days after the Court has ...
-- decided that no further information ... is reasonably required."  An "until" bar, its own yes/no (ruling of 2026-11-05 (d)):
-- JUST TRUE while the time cannot yet expire, JUST FALSE after.  "(a) or (b)" is read as whichever comes first (a fork).
-- FORK: whichever of (a) and (b) comes first ends the bar / other: both must have happened.
-- src:6913 | (3) Where a defendant gives a notice under this Rule, the time limited for filing and serving a notice of intention to contest or not contest does not expire until — ( a )
-- src:6914 | 4 days after the defendant has notified the claimant in writing that the defendant is satisfied with the statement supplied in compliance with the notice; or
-- src:6915 | ( b )
-- src:6916 | 4 days after the Court has, on the application of the claimant by summons served on the defendant at least 14 days before the return day, decided that no further information as to the matters referred to in paragraph (1) is reasonably required.
@ref O 59 r 2(3) (src:6913-6916)
GIVEN `the defendant gave a notice under this Rule` IS A BOOLEAN
      `o 3 r 3 the last day of the 4 days after the defendant's written notification, if there was one` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `o 3 r 3 the last day of the 4 days after the Court's decision, if there was one` IS A MAYBE DATE
      `the date being asked about` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 2(3) the time for filing a notice of intention to contest or not contest cannot yet expire` MEANS
    IF NOT (`the defendant gave a notice under this Rule`)
    THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Where a defendant gives a notice under this Rule"
    ELSE CONSIDER `o 3 r 3 the last day of the 4 days after the defendant's written notification, if there was one`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN
            CONSIDER `o 3 r 3 the last day of the 4 days after the Court's decision, if there was one`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN JUST TRUE
            WHEN JUST courtEnd THEN IF `the date being asked about` AT MOST courtEnd THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "does not expire until"
        WHEN JUST notifiedEnd THEN
            CONSIDER `o 3 r 3 the last day of the 4 days after the Court's decision, if there was one`
            WHEN NOTHING THEN IF `the date being asked about` AT MOST notifiedEnd THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "does not expire until"
            WHEN JUST courtEnd THEN IF `the date being asked about` AT MOST (IF notifiedEnd AT MOST courtEnd THEN notifiedEnd ELSE courtEnd) THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "does not expire until"

-- (3)(b): the claimant's summons, "served on the defendant at least 14 days before the return day".
`o 59 r 2(3)(b) the period for serving the summons before the return day` MEANS Period WITH
    `period length` IS 14
    `period unit` IS Days
    `period direction` IS `Before the anchor`

-- src:6915 | ( b )
-- src:6916 | 4 days after the Court has, on the application of the claimant by summons served on the defendant at least 14 days before the return day, decided that no further information as to the matters referred to in paragraph (1) is reasonably required.
@ref O 59 r 2(3) (src:6915-6916)
GIVEN document IS A `Document kind`
      `the summons is served on the defendant` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the date it was served` IS A DATE
      `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 2(3)(b) the claimant's application is made and served as the Rule requires` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the date it was served` GREATER THAN `o 3 r 3 the last day for doing the act`
        THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "served on the defendant at least 14 days before the return day"
        IF document EQUALS Summons AND `the summons is served on the defendant`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "by summons served on the defendant" is the way the sub-paragraph gives for the application, and a bare "by summons"; QB: NOTHING (O 3 r 2(4)) / other: JUST FALSE


