    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): rule 3(2) speaks of a document required to be served on the Government in connection with civil proceedings

-- src:6918 | 3. —(1) Order 7, Order 8 and any other provision of these Rules relating to service out of Singapore do not apply in relation to the service of any process by which civil proceedings against the Government are begun. (2) Personal service of any document required to be served on the Government for the purpose of or in connection with any civil proceedings is not requisite; but where the proceedings are by or against the Government, service on the Government must be effected — ( a )
-- src:6919 | by leaving the document at the office of the person who is in accordance with section 20 to be served, or of any agent whom that person has nominated for the purpose, but in either case with a member of the staff of that person or agent; or
-- src:6920 | ( b )
-- src:6921 | by posting it in a prepaid envelope addressed to the person who is in accordance with section 20 to be served or to any such agent.
-- src:6922 | (3) In relation to the service of any document required to be served on the Government for the purpose of or in connection with any civil proceedings by or against the Government, Order 7, Rule 3 does not apply, and Order 7, Rule 8 applies to service under paragraph (2) of this Rule.
@ref O 59 r 3(2) (src:6918-6922)
GIVEN `the proceedings are by or against the Government` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the document is left at the office of the person to be served in accordance with section 20, or of an agent that person has nominated, with a member of the staff of that person or agent` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the document is posted in a prepaid envelope addressed to that person or to such an agent` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 3(2) service on the Government is effected as the Rule requires` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the proceedings are by or against the Government`)
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the requirement applies "where the proceedings are by or against the Government"
        IF `the document is left at the office of the person to be served in accordance with section 20, or of an agent that person has nominated, with a member of the staff of that person or agent` OR `the document is posted in a prepaid envelope addressed to that person or to such an agent`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): a bare "service on the Government must be effected (a) ... or (b) ..."; QB: NOTHING (O 3 r 2(4)) / other: JUST FALSE

-- (3): "Order 7, Rule 3 does not apply, and Order 7, Rule 8 applies to service under paragraph (2)."
-- src:6922 | (3) In relation to the service of any document required to be served on the Government for the purpose of or in connection with any civil proceedings by or against the Government, Order 7, Rule 3 does not apply, and Order 7, Rule 8 applies to service under paragraph (2) of this Rule.
@ref O 59 r 3(3) (src:6922)
GIVEN `the document is required to be served on the Government for the purpose of or in connection with civil proceedings by or against the Government` IS A BOOLEAN
      provision IS A STRING
      `the service is under paragraph (2)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 3(3) the provision applies to the service` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the document is required to be served on the Government for the purpose of or in connection with civil proceedings by or against the Government`)
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): rule 3(3) speaks of the service of "any document required to be served on the Government for the purpose of or in connection with any civil proceedings by or against the Government"
        IF provision EQUALS "Order 7 rule 3"
        THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Order 7, Rule 3 does not apply"
        IF provision EQUALS "Order 7 rule 8" AND `the service is under paragraph (2)`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): only Order 7 rules 3 and 8 are named, and rule 8 only for service under paragraph (2)

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Rule 4 -- counterclaim and set-off`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- (1): flat negative.  The bearer is "a person" in proceedings by the Government (the defendant), not a named actor: no SHANT is derived.
-- src:6924 | 4. —(1) Despite Order 6, Rules 8 and 14, a person may not in any proceedings by the Government make any counterclaim or plead a set‑off if the proceedings are for the recovery of, or the counterclaim or set‑off arises out of a right or claim to repayment in respect of, any taxes, duties or penalties. (2) Despite Order 6, Rules 8 and 14, no counterclaim may be made, or set‑off pleaded, without the permission of the Court, by the Government in proceedings against the Government, or by any person in proceedings by the Government — ( a )
@ref O 59 r 4(1) (src:6924)
GIVEN `the proceedings are proceedings by the Government` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the proceedings are for the recovery of any taxes, duties or penalties` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the counterclaim or set-off arises out of a right or claim to repayment in respect of any taxes, duties or penalties` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 4(1) a person may make a counterclaim or plead a set-off` MEANS
    IF `the proceedings are proceedings by the Government` AND (`the proceedings are for the recovery of any taxes, duties or penalties` OR `the counterclaim or set-off arises out of a right or claim to repayment in respect of any taxes, duties or penalties`)
    THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a person may not in any proceedings by the Government make any counterclaim or plead a set-off if the proceedings are for the recovery of, or the counterclaim or set-off arises out of a right or claim to repayment in respect of, any taxes, duties or penalties"
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the bar names counterclaims and set-offs in proceedings by the Government that concern taxes, duties or penalties, and the Rule does not say that others may be made

-- (2): "no counterclaim may be made, or set-off pleaded, without the permission of the Court, by the Government in proceedings
-- against the Government, or by any person in proceedings by the Government -- (a) if the Government is sued or sues in the name of
-- a Government department and the subject matter ... does not relate to that department; or (b) if the Government is sued or sues in
-- the name of the Attorney-General."
DECLARE `o 59 r 4(2) who makes it and in what proceedings` IS ONE OF
    `o 59 r 4(2) the Government in proceedings against the Government`
    `o 59 r 4(2) a person in proceedings by the Government`
    `o 59 r 4(2) someone else`

DECLARE `o 59 r 4(2) in whose name the Government is sued or sues` IS ONE OF
    `o 59 r 4(2) a Government department`
    `o 59 r 4(2) the Attorney-General`
    `o 59 r 4(2) another name`

-- The requirement applies: JUST TRUE; the "if" conditions (a) and (b) unmet: JUST FALSE (a failed "if", marginal reading); the
-- who-and-where scope unmet: NOTHING (a scope limit, ruling (c)).
-- src:6924 | 4. —(1) Despite Order 6, Rules 8 and 14, a person may not in any proceedings by the Government make any counterclaim or plead a set‑off if the proceedings are for the recovery of, or the counterclaim or set‑off arises out of a right or claim to repayment in respect of, any taxes, duties or penalties. (2) Despite Order 6, Rules 8 and 14, no counterclaim may be made, or set‑off pleaded, without the permission of the Court, by the Government in proceedings against the Government, or by any person in proceedings by the Government — ( a )
-- src:6925 | if the Government is sued or sues in the name of a Government department and the subject matter of the counterclaim or set‑off does not relate to that department; or
-- src:6926 | ( b )
-- src:6927 | if the Government is sued or sues in the name of the Attorney‑General.
-- src:6928 | (3) Any application for permission under this Rule must be made by summons.
@ref O 59 r 4(2) (src:6924-6928)
GIVEN who IS A `o 59 r 4(2) who makes it and in what proceedings`
      name IS A `o 59 r 4(2) in whose name the Government is sued or sues`
      `the subject matter of the counterclaim or set-off relates to that department` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 4(2) the permission of the Court is required for the counterclaim or set-off` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF who EQUALS `o 59 r 4(2) someone else`
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): rule 4(2) speaks of a counterclaim or set-off "by the Government in proceedings against the Government, or by any person in proceedings by the Government"
        IF name EQUALS `o 59 r 4(2) a Government department` AND NOT (`the subject matter of the counterclaim or set-off relates to that department`)
        THEN JUST TRUE
        IF name EQUALS `o 59 r 4(2) the Attorney-General`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if the Government is sued or sues in the name of a Government department and the subject matter of the counterclaim or set-off does not relate to that department"

-- The permission question.  QA: "without the permission of the Court" once permission is given: NOTHING (a lifted bar grants nothing)
-- / other: JUST TRUE (the exception permits it).  The failing side stays JUST FALSE.
-- src:6924 | 4. —(1) Despite Order 6, Rules 8 and 14, a person may not in any proceedings by the Government make any counterclaim or plead a set‑off if the proceedings are for the recovery of, or the counterclaim or set‑off arises out of a right or claim to repayment in respect of, any taxes, duties or penalties. (2) Despite Order 6, Rules 8 and 14, no counterclaim may be made, or set‑off pleaded, without the permission of the Court, by the Government in proceedings against the Government, or by any person in proceedings by the Government — ( a )
-- src:6925 | if the Government is sued or sues in the name of a Government department and the subject matter of the counterclaim or set‑off does not relate to that department; or
-- src:6926 | ( b )
-- src:6927 | if the Government is sued or sues in the name of the Attorney‑General.
-- src:6928 | (3) Any application for permission under this Rule must be made by summons.
@ref O 59 r 4(2) (src:6924-6928)
GIVEN who IS A `o 59 r 4(2) who makes it and in what proceedings`
      name IS A `o 59 r 4(2) in whose name the Government is sued or sues`
      `the subject matter of the counterclaim or set-off relates to that department` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Court has given permission` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 4(2) the counterclaim may be made or the set-off pleaded` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`o 59 r 4(2) the permission of the Court is required for the counterclaim or set-off` who name `the subject matter of the counterclaim or set-off relates to that department` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the bar of rule 4(2) does not arise on these facts, and a bar that does not arise grants no permission
        IF `the Court has given permission`
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the bar is lifted by the permission, and a lifted bar grants nothing; QA: NOTHING / other: JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "no counterclaim may be made, or set-off pleaded, without the permission of the Court"

-- src:6928 | (3) Any application for permission under this Rule must be made by summons.
@ref O 59 r 4(3) (src:6928)
GIVEN `the application is one for permission under this Rule` IS A BOOLEAN
      document IS A `Document kind`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 4(3) the application for permission is made as the Rule requires` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the application is one for permission under this Rule`)
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): rule 4(3) speaks of "Any application for permission under this Rule"
        IF document EQUALS Summons
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): a bare "must be made by summons"; QB: NOTHING (O 3 r 2(4)) / other: JUST FALSE

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Rule 5 -- summary judgment`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

DECLARE `o 59 r 5(1) kind of proceedings` IS ONE OF
    `o 59 r 5(1) proceedings against the Government`
    `o 59 r 5(1) proceedings by the Government`

-- (1): "No application against the Government may be made (a) under Order 9, Rule 17 or 19, in any proceedings against the Government;
-- or (b) under Order 9, Rule 17(11) or 19, in any proceedings by the Government."  A flat negative about O 9 (S-L2).
-- src:6930 | 5. —(1) No application against the Government may be made — ( a )
-- src:6931 | under Order 9, Rule 17 or 19, in any proceedings against the Government; or
-- src:6932 | ( b )
-- src:6933 | under Order 9, Rule 17(11) or 19, in any proceedings by the Government.
-- src:6934 | (2) Where an application is made by the Government under Order 9, Rule 17(1) or (11) or 19, the affidavit required in support of the application must be made by — ( a )
@ref O 59 r 5(1) (src:6930-6934)
GIVEN kind IS A `o 59 r 5(1) kind of proceedings`
      provision IS A STRING
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 5(1) an application against the Government may be made under the provision` MEANS
    CONSIDER kind
    WHEN `o 59 r 5(1) proceedings against the Government` THEN
        IF provision EQUALS "Order 9 rule 17" OR provision EQUALS "Order 9 rule 19"
        THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "No application against the Government may be made"
        ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): only Order 9 rules 17 and 19 are named for proceedings against the Government
    WHEN `o 59 r 5(1) proceedings by the Government` THEN
        IF provision EQUALS "Order 9 rule 17(11)" OR provision EQUALS "Order 9 rule 19"
        THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "under Order 9, Rule 17(11) or 19, in any proceedings by the Government"
        ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): only Order 9 rules 17(11) and 19 are named for proceedings by the Government

DECLARE `o 59 r 5(2) deponent` IS ONE OF
    `o 59 r 5(2)(a) the solicitor acting for the Government`
    `o 59 r 5(2)(b) an officer duly authorised by the solicitor so acting`
    `o 59 r 5(2)(b) an officer duly authorised by the department concerned`
    `o 59 r 5(2) someone else`

-- src:6934 | (2) Where an application is made by the Government under Order 9, Rule 17(1) or (11) or 19, the affidavit required in support of the application must be made by — ( a )
-- src:6935 | the solicitor acting for the Government; or
-- src:6936 | ( b )
-- src:6937 | an officer duly authorised by the solicitor so acting or by the department concerned.
@ref O 59 r 5(2) (src:6934-6937)
GIVEN `the application is made by the Government under Order 9 rule 17(1) or (11) or 19` IS A BOOLEAN
      deponent IS A `o 59 r 5(2) deponent`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 5(2) the affidavit in support is made by a person the Rule allows` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the application is made by the Government under Order 9 rule 17(1) or (11) or 19`)
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): rule 5(2) speaks of an application "made by the Government under Order 9, Rule 17(1) or (11) or 19"
        IF deponent EQUALS `o 59 r 5(2) someone else`
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): a bare "must be made by (a) ... or (b) ..."; QB: NOTHING (O 3 r 2(4)) / other: JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
§§ `Rule 6 -- summary applications to Court in certain revenue matters`
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- src:6939 | 6. —(1) This Rule applies to applications under section 21. (2) An application to which this Rule applies must be made by originating application.
@ref O 59 r 6(1) (src:6939)
GIVEN `the application is one under section 21` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 6(1) this Rule applies to the application` MEANS
    IF `the application is one under section 21`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "This Rule applies to applications under section 21" names the applications it reaches and says nothing of others

-- src:6939 | 6. —(1) This Rule applies to applications under section 21. (2) An application to which this Rule applies must be made by originating application.
@ref O 59 r 6(2) (src:6939)
GIVEN `this Rule applies to the application` IS A BOOLEAN
      document IS A `Document kind`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 6(2) the application is made as the Rule requires` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`this Rule applies to the application`)
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): rule 6(2) speaks of "An application to which this Rule applies"
        IF document EQUALS `Originating application`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): a bare "must be made by originating application"; QB: NOTHING (O 3 r 2(4)) / other: JUST FALSE

-- src:6940 | (3) The person from whom any account or information or payment is claimed or by whom any books are required to be produced must be made defendant to the application.
@ref O 59 r 6(3) (src:6940)
GIVEN `the person is the one from whom any account or information or payment is claimed or by whom any books are required to be produced` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is made defendant to the application` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 6(3) the person is made defendant as the Rule requires` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the person is the one from whom any account or information or payment is claimed or by whom any books are required to be produced`)
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): rule 6(3) speaks of "The person from whom any account or information or payment is claimed or by whom any books are required to be produced"
        IF `the person is made defendant to the application`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): a bare "must be made defendant"; QB: NOTHING (O 3 r 2(4)) / other: JUST FALSE

-- (4): what the originating application must do.  "where information is claimed" makes (b)'s second limb conditional.
DECLARE `o 59 r 6(4) originating application` HAS
    `o 59 r 6(4)(a) is entitled in the matter or matters out of which the need for the application arises` IS A BOOLEAN
    `o 59 r 6(4)(a) is entitled in the matter of the Act` IS A BOOLEAN
    `o 59 r 6(4)(b) refers to the written law under which the account or information or payment or the production of books is claimed` IS A BOOLEAN
    `o 59 r 6(4)(b) information is claimed` IS A BOOLEAN
    `o 59 r 6(4)(b) shows by appropriate questions or otherwise what information is required` IS A BOOLEAN

-- src:6941 | (4) An originating application under this Rule — ( a )
-- src:6942 | must be entitled in the matter or matters out of which the need for the application arises and in the matter of the Act; and
-- src:6943 | ( b )
-- src:6944 | must refer to the written law under which the account or information or payment or the production of books is claimed and, where information is claimed, must show (by appropriate questions or otherwise) what information is required.
-- src:6945 | (5) Upon any application to which this Rule applies, an affidavit by a duly authorised officer of the Government department concerned setting out the state of facts upon which the application is based and stating that he or she has reason to think that those facts exist is evidence of those facts; and if evidence is filed disputing any of those facts, further evidence may be filed, and the Court may either decide the matter upon the affidavits (after any cross‑examination that may have been ordered) or may direct that it be decided by oral evidence in Court.
@ref O 59 r 6(4) (src:6941-6945)
GIVEN application IS A `o 59 r 6(4) originating application`
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`o 59 r 6(4) the matters the originating application has not yet satisfied` MEANS
    concat (LIST
        (IF application's `o 59 r 6(4)(a) is entitled in the matter or matters out of which the need for the application arises` THEN EMPTY ELSE LIST "(a) entitled in the matter or matters out of which the need for the application arises"),
        (IF application's `o 59 r 6(4)(a) is entitled in the matter of the Act` THEN EMPTY ELSE LIST "(a) entitled in the matter of the Act"),
        (IF application's `o 59 r 6(4)(b) refers to the written law under which the account or information or payment or the production of books is claimed` THEN EMPTY ELSE LIST "(b) refer to the written law under which the account or information or payment or the production of books is claimed"),
        (IF (NOT (application's `o 59 r 6(4)(b) information is claimed`)) OR application's `o 59 r 6(4)(b) shows by appropriate questions or otherwise what information is required` THEN EMPTY ELSE LIST "(b) where information is claimed, show (by appropriate questions or otherwise) what information is required"))

-- src:6941 | (4) An originating application under this Rule — ( a )
-- src:6942 | must be entitled in the matter or matters out of which the need for the application arises and in the matter of the Act; and
-- src:6943 | ( b )
-- src:6944 | must refer to the written law under which the account or information or payment or the production of books is claimed and, where information is claimed, must show (by appropriate questions or otherwise) what information is required.
-- src:6945 | (5) Upon any application to which this Rule applies, an affidavit by a duly authorised officer of the Government department concerned setting out the state of facts upon which the application is based and stating that he or she has reason to think that those facts exist is evidence of those facts; and if evidence is filed disputing any of those facts, further evidence may be filed, and the Court may either decide the matter upon the affidavits (after any cross‑examination that may have been ordered) or may direct that it be decided by oral evidence in Court.
@ref O 59 r 6(4) (src:6941-6945)
GIVEN `this Rule applies to the application` IS A BOOLEAN
      application IS A `o 59 r 6(4) originating application`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 6(4) the originating application is what the Rule requires` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`this Rule applies to the application`)
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): rule 6(4) speaks of "An originating application under this Rule"
        IF count (`o 59 r 6(4) the matters the originating application has not yet satisfied` application) EQUALS 0
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): a bare "must be entitled ... and must refer to"; QB: NOTHING (O 3 r 2(4)) / other: JUST FALSE

-- (5): the officer's affidavit is evidence.
-- src:6945 | (5) Upon any application to which this Rule applies, an affidavit by a duly authorised officer of the Government department concerned setting out the state of facts upon which the application is based and stating that he or she has reason to think that those facts exist is evidence of those facts; and if evidence is filed disputing any of those facts, further evidence may be filed, and the Court may either decide the matter upon the affidavits (after any cross‑examination that may have been ordered) or may direct that it be decided by oral evidence in Court.
-- src:6946 | (6) An order in favour of the Government on an application to which this Rule applies must, unless the Court otherwise determines, name a time within which each of its terms is to be complied with.
@ref O 59 r 6(5) (src:6945-6946)
GIVEN `this Rule applies to the application` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the affidavit is by a duly authorised officer of the Government department concerned` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it sets out the state of facts upon which the application is based` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it states that the officer has reason to think that those facts exist` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 6(5) the affidavit is evidence of those facts` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`this Rule applies to the application`)
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): rule 6(5) speaks of "any application to which this Rule applies"
        IF `the affidavit is by a duly authorised officer of the Government department concerned` AND `it sets out the state of facts upon which the application is based` AND `it states that the officer has reason to think that those facts exist`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the Rule makes an affidavit of that description evidence and says nothing of any other affidavit

DECLARE `o 59 r 6(5) course` IS ONE OF
    `o 59 r 6(5) further evidence is filed`
    `o 59 r 6(5) the Court decides the matter upon the affidavits`
    `o 59 r 6(5) the Court directs that the matter be decided by oral evidence in Court`

-- "and if evidence is filed disputing any of those facts, further evidence may be filed, and the Court may either decide the matter
-- upon the affidavits ... or may direct that it be decided by oral evidence in Court": a failed "if" is JUST FALSE.
-- src:6945 | (5) Upon any application to which this Rule applies, an affidavit by a duly authorised officer of the Government department concerned setting out the state of facts upon which the application is based and stating that he or she has reason to think that those facts exist is evidence of those facts; and if evidence is filed disputing any of those facts, further evidence may be filed, and the Court may either decide the matter upon the affidavits (after any cross‑examination that may have been ordered) or may direct that it be decided by oral evidence in Court.
-- src:6946 | (6) An order in favour of the Government on an application to which this Rule applies must, unless the Court otherwise determines, name a time within which each of its terms is to be complied with.
@ref O 59 r 6(5) (src:6945-6946)
GIVEN course IS A `o 59 r 6(5) course`
      `evidence is filed disputing any of those facts` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 6(5) the course is open` MEANS
    IF `evidence is filed disputing any of those facts`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if evidence is filed disputing any of those facts"

-- (6): "unless the Court otherwise determines": a duty lifted by an exception (the requirement does not apply: JUST FALSE).
-- src:6946 | (6) An order in favour of the Government on an application to which this Rule applies must, unless the Court otherwise determines, name a time within which each of its terms is to be complied with.
-- src:6947 | (7) Nothing in this Rule, in relation to any case in which the only relief claimed by the Government is the payment of money, is to be construed as requiring the Government to proceed by way of an application to which this Rule applies or as preventing the Government from availing itself of any other procedure which is open to it under these Rules.
@ref O 59 r 6(6) (src:6946-6947)
GIVEN `the order is in favour of the Government on an application to which this Rule applies` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Court otherwise determines` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 6(6) the duty to name a time for compliance with each term arises` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`the order is in favour of the Government on an application to which this Rule applies`)
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): rule 6(6) speaks of "An order in favour of the Government on an application to which this Rule applies"
        IF `the Court otherwise determines`
        THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless the Court otherwise determines"
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- src:6946 | (6) An order in favour of the Government on an application to which this Rule applies must, unless the Court otherwise determines, name a time within which each of its terms is to be complied with.
-- src:6947 | (7) Nothing in this Rule, in relation to any case in which the only relief claimed by the Government is the payment of money, is to be construed as requiring the Government to proceed by way of an application to which this Rule applies or as preventing the Government from availing itself of any other procedure which is open to it under these Rules.
@ref O 59 r 6(6) (src:6946-6947)
GIVEN `the order is in favour of the Government on an application to which this Rule applies` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the Court otherwise determines` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the order names a time within which each of its terms is to be complied with` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 6(6) the order names a time as the Rule requires` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT (`o 59 r 6(6) the duty to name a time for compliance with each term arises` `the order is in favour of the Government on an application to which this Rule applies` `the Court otherwise determines` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
        THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the duty does not arise; QD: NOTHING, and the companion `o 59 r 6(6) the duty to name a time for compliance with each term arises` answers JUST FALSE or NOTHING / other: JUST TRUE (nothing is unmet)
        IF `the order names a time within which each of its terms is to be complied with`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): a bare "must ... name a time"; QB: NOTHING (O 3 r 2(4)) / other: JUST FALSE

-- (7): "Nothing in this Rule, in relation to any case in which the only relief claimed by the Government is the payment of money, is
-- to be construed as requiring the Government to proceed by way of an application ... or as preventing the Government from availing
-- itself of any other procedure": two flat negatives about this Rule.
-- src:6947 | (7) Nothing in this Rule, in relation to any case in which the only relief claimed by the Government is the payment of money, is to be construed as requiring the Government to proceed by way of an application to which this Rule applies or as preventing the Government from availing itself of any other procedure which is open to it under these Rules.
@ref O 59 r 6(7) (src:6947)
GIVEN `the only relief claimed by the Government is the payment of money` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`o 59 r 6(7) this Rule requires the Government to proceed by way of an application under it` MEANS
    IF `the only relief claimed by the Government is the payment of money`
    THEN JUST FALSE -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "is to be construed as requiring the Government to proceed by way of an application to which this Rule applies"
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): rule 6(7) speaks of a case "in which the only relief claimed by the Government is the payment of money"

-- src:6947 | (7) Nothing in this Rule, in relation to any case in which the only relief claimed by the Government is the payment of money, is to be construed as requiring the Government to proceed by way of an application to which this Rule applies or as preventing the Government from availing itself of any other procedure which is open to it under these Rules.
@ref O 59 r 6(7) (src:6947)
GIVEN `the only relief claimed by the Government is the payment of money` IS A BOOLEAN
